# Retained Edge Counting

The narrow counted strategy for runtime-many persistent retained edges.

**REC is a physical representation, not source semantics. Moth has no source-visible reference counting, and no Moth program can name, observe or request a counter.**

## Contract

- Retained-edge analysis supplies: allocation-family candidates, retained-edge creation and destruction facts, cardinality, cleanup-frontier candidates and builtin collection effects
- The memory-strategy planner supplies: the selected `REC` strategy or an explicit REC-elision decision
- REC lowering produces: counted-family layout, tags, count transitions and iterative destruction plans for REC-selected families
- Decides: how an already-selected REC family carries retain, release and extraction operations and how counted destruction runs
- Must not decide: source legality, lifetime ownership, retained-edge legality, cycle legality, memory-strategy selection or whether a program compiles
- Invariant: REC counts persistent retained edges only. It never counts ordinary aliases, never establishes legality and never permits cycles

The ownership chain stays one-way:

```text
retained-edge analysis
    -> candidate and cardinality facts
memory-strategy planner
    -> REC selected or elided
REC lowering
    -> tags, counter layout, transitions and destruction plan
```

Every REC family keeps its statically proven fallback lifetime region. REC can only reclaim that family earlier than the fallback region frontier. If counting did not exist or failed to improve precision, the static region would still make the program collector-free and correct. The planner fixes the representation before lowering: an uncounted strategy never upgrades to REC at runtime, and an REC-selected strategy never abandons counting or downgrades to an uncounted representation at runtime.

REC is selected only after affine cleanup, inferred regions, cleanup frontiers, explicit groups, field-sensitive splitting and cheap bounded retention have all failed to give a precise enough answer. It is a precision mechanism inside a statically safe collector-free system, not the correctness fallback.

Implementation of this analysis is deferred.

